ons to see if  there's a greater inclusion and what  came out of the meeting what came out of  the meeting was a very interesting  structure of how we should be thinking  about the evidence from the different  sectors that address kids health  education legal systems justice but also  looking at the evidence from faith-based  organizations NGOs private sector and  how can we put these together to really  understand what is the strongest  provisions for children and how are we  in some ways maybe not attentive to a  fullest potential if we cut up the work  in this multiple ways so we have a we  have a ways to go but this group sort of  came together and thinking together this  framework in over the next couple of  months actually by next year we should  have a pretty authoritative volume out  on this one you can use for a high-level  launch and so what do you think is the  most important thing that needs to  happen to help child rights and policy  that's there multiple parts to this  answer I think just because early  childhood development is very complex in  that it really brings together multiple  sectors and agencies who are working  towards children's development and sort  of the economic development of a country  so for the most I would sort of say  they're pieces to this that come  together in an interesting tapestry but  then there isn't sort of a linear  arrangement around it in in my  experience and in sort of working with  now of 40 odd countries around the world  with governments national governments on  improving conditions for young children  one piece I I find that is quite  remarkable is oftentimes parents or key  caregivers in the  we are not aware of the importance of  these early years and that learning does  begin at birth and that everything we do  with young children enhances their  capacity and their ability and their  potential I mean there is a universal  recognition of wanting your children to  be healthy to be happy I mean I've done  focus groups and remote at with remote  populations in Laos I've done folks  trips and families and sort of very  educated in Singapore and they'll all  you ask them what do you want for your  children and they all say we want  healthy children we want to be happy to  be successful but once you start going  back and saying how do you think this  happens and what could you do that's  when we notice there is a knowledge gap  and that's really what we need to work  on I think if somehow there was ways  there and there are ways to really get  the word out about the importance of the  early years about immunization about  health about breastfeeding families will  start demanding that and once families  communities public will build to demand  from the government and we see what  demand can do recently we're seeing a  lot of yes once a population starts  demanding what they need we will see  service provision change we will see  policies responsive to that but  education really is the first step is  the first step and there is a very key  link between particularly maternal  education maternal understanding and  child well-being another big piece to  this is the capacity to deliver programs  and deliver these services in most  countries the problem with scaling up is  they're not sufficient trained  individuals to actually take it forward  right and so the quality of the work and  the effectiveness of the program starts  dropping and if we had systems to  actually enhance capacity to build  training models we could reach a large  number of population families children  we are not reaching now and the the  advantage to all of this is we have  technology no longer are we bound into  classrooms  walls have exploded have expanded and  through technology there are multiple  ways we can really build build capacity  and finally I think we need stronger  arguments that really link this  investment in the early years and  economic development of countries we  have it we have the data that